title: The End of the Revolutionary Year
new-page: true
on-arrival: {!
window.RF.normalize(Q);
// grade: 2 = triumph, 1 = partial, 0 = defeat
if (Q.rf_ending == 'revolution') {
    Q.grade = Q.player_leads ? 2 : 1;
} else if (Q.rf_ending == 'frankfurt') {
    if (Q.player_leads) {
        Q.grade = 2;
    } else if (Q.player_strength >= 25 || (Q.shared_leadership && Q.player_strength >= 15)) {
        Q.grade = 1;
    } else {
        Q.grade = 0;
    }
} else {
    Q.grade = 0;
}
!}
go-to: revolution if rf_ending == "revolution"; frankfurt if rf_ending == "frankfurt"; splinter if rf_ending == "splinter"; leave if rf_ending == "leave"

@revolution
new-page: true

= The German October

[? if grade = 2 : **Triumph.** ?][? if grade = 1 : **A revolution, but not yours.** ?]

Germany has a Council Republic. The Reich government has fled south; the Reichswehr is split; Bavaria and the occupied Rhineland are in other hands. Soviet Russia sends grain and advisers across a Poland that may not let them pass. The civil war is only beginning.

[? if grade = 2 : The [+ player_name +] leads the revolution it fought for. ?][? if grade = 1 : The revolution is led by the [+ leader_name +]. Your faction will have to fight for its place in the new state. ?]

In our history, almost every historian of 1923 judges that a German October could not have won: the Reichswehr was loyal, the SPD's workers did not follow, and the party's weapons existed mostly in its reports to Moscow. You beat those odds.

- @finish: End game.

@frankfurt
new-page: true

= Frankfurt, April 1924

[? if grade = 2 : **Victory: the [+ player_name +] leads the party.** ?][? if grade = 1 : **Survival: the [+ player_name +] remains a force in the party.** ?][? if grade = 0 : **Defeat: the [+ player_name +] is broken.** ?]

The revolutionary year is over. The KPD has about [+ members +] thousand members and is led by the [+ leader_name +]. Moscow favours the [+ favourite_name +], and the party's autonomy stands at [+ autonomy +] out of 100.

[? if player == "right" and grade = 2 : Against the current, you have kept the leadership. Zinoviev will not forgive it, and the Left will not stop. But the united front is still the party's policy. ?]
[? if player == "right" and grade < 2 : In our history, Brandler and Thalheimer were kept in Moscow from 1924 until 1928. When they came home, they opposed the Third Period line, were expelled, and founded the KPO. ?]
[? if player == "left" and grade = 2 : The party is yours, as it was historically. Zinoviev is your patron, and Zinoviev is losing his struggle in Moscow. The next blow will come from the same International that made you. ?]
[? if player == "left" and grade < 2 : In our history the Left won at Frankfurt. Here, you have lost the moment when the party's anger was on your side. ?]
[? if player == "center" and grade = 2 : The party is still whole and you hold it together. Every faction resents you a little, which may be the definition of success. ?]
[? if player == "center" and grade < 2 : The Middle Group's caretaker moment has passed. In our history its leaders survived by attaching themselves to whoever won. ?]
[? if player == "stalin" and grade = 2 : Years early, Thälmann's circle controls the party. Moscow has learned it can rely on you. The question for later acts is whether that loyalty will be worth anything when the Nazis come. ?]
[? if player == "stalin" and grade < 2 : Your time has not come. In our history it came in 1925, when the Comintern's Open Letter removed Fischer and Maslow and made Thälmann chairman. ?]

Final standings: Brandler Right [+ right_strength +]%, Left [+ left_strength +]%, Middle Group [+ center_strength +]%, Thälmann circle [+ stalin_strength +]%.

This is the end of the prototype. Acts II to V (Bolshevization, stabilisation, the Third Period, and 1930 to 1934) are not built yet.

- @finish: End game.

@splinter
new-page: true

= A new party

**Expelled.** You and your comrades found a new party. You keep your principles and lose the International, its money and the loyalty that the name "Communist Party" commands among German workers. In our history, the splinters of the KPD (the KAPD, the Leninbund, the KPO) never won more than a small fraction of the party's vote.

In a later version of the game, this would be a playable splinter mode.

- @finish: End game.

@leave
new-page: true

= Leaving

**Expelled.** You leave the Communist movement. Some who took this road, like Paul Levi in 1921, joined the SPD and spent the rest of their lives arguing that the KPD had wasted the German working class's best chance.

- @finish: End game.

@finish
game-over: true
